/*
 * EmitsArray.ag — §7.63's `Array<T>`, which is an ALIAS and not a construct.
 *
 * Copyright © 2026 Kyle J. Cardoza. MIT licence (see docs/LICENSE).
 *
 * The name resolves to one of TWO types by its *argument*, and the two differ
 * in every way an author can observe — so pinning one of them pins nothing. The
 * two are `Array<Int32>` = `int32_t const *` (a value view) and `Array<String>`
 * = `NSArray<NSString *> *` (exactly the ObjC type).
 *
 * THE OBJECT FAMILY IS NOT PINNED HERE YET, and that is a measured state rather
 * than an oversight: clang refuses `NSArray<NSString *>` outright today because
 * no collection in this tree's Foundation declares generic parameters — "type
 * arguments cannot be applied to non-parameterized class 'NSArray'". So the
 * object form refuses BY NAME (tests/refuse/array-object.ag holds it), and this
 * specimen pins the family that ships: the VALUE.
 *
 * The value family's other two members are here as well — a DECLARED STRUCT and
 * a DECLARED ENUM, which are §4's values and not names §4's table holds, so the
 * classifier has to reach this file's own declarations to see them. A struct's
 * fields are never named: the element type is the outer name.
 *
 * §5's MEMORY QUALIFIER IS WHY A GOLDEN IS REQUIRED AND NOT A PROBE — for the
 * object form, which is the one that will need it: `Array<String>` is an OBJECT
 * whose name is not a class, so asking "is the name a class" answers no and
 * emits `@property (nonatomic, assign) NSArray<NSString *> *names;`, which
 * COMPILES, does not retain the array, and reads as an ordinary declaration.
 * `type_is_reference` is the question that fixes it, and it is exercised here by
 * the value family staying `assign`.
 *
 * The two `func`s carry the alias through a PARAMETER and a RESULT, which is a
 * second code path (the declaration printer) and where a dropped argument would
 * show. They are `func`s and not `method`s deliberately: §7.1 capitalises a
 * first parameter's external name onto the method name (`take` + `items` reads
 * `takeItems:`), §7.50 makes `_` suppress the label, and the emitter does
 * neither yet — `take(items:)` comes out `take:`, `take(_ items:)` comes out
 * `take_:`, and `take(from items:)` comes out `takefrom:`. Pinning a method here
 * would assert a spelling already measured to contradict §7.1; a `func` has no
 * selector at all and so asserts nothing about one. The selector gap is recorded
 * in the plan rather than encoded in this golden.
 */
enum EmitsArrayLevel: Int32 {
    case low = 1
    case high
}

struct EmitsArraySpan {
    var lo: Int32
    var hi: Int32
}

func EmitsArrayTotal(values: Array<Int32>) -> Int32 {
    return 0;
}

func EmitsArrayScale(factors: Array<Float64>) -> Array<Float64> {
    return factors;
}

class EmitsArray: Object {
    property numbers: Array<Int32>
    property levels: Array<EmitsArrayLevel>
    property spans: Array<EmitsArraySpan>
    property maybeNumbers: Array<Int32>?
}
